## Usage

```bash
pip install {{ pypi_name }}
meds-extract-run spec={{ dataset_name }} output_dir=$MEDS_OUTPUT_DIR
```

{% if has_demo -%}
To run against the smaller open demo release instead, add `dataset_key=demo`.

{% endif -%}
Raw data is downloaded as the `sources:` block of the packaged spec says; set any credentials as
environment variables. To run on raw data you already have, add `do_download=false input_dir=$RAW_INPUT_DIR`.
The output is a MEDS dataset (`data/` and `metadata/`, per the
[MEDS standard](https://medical-event-data-standard.github.io/)), with the dataset name and version
recorded in `metadata/dataset.json`.

## How this ETL is defined

The whole ETL is one MESSY file,
[`src/{{ package_name }}/event_configs.yaml`](src/{{ package_name }}/event_configs.yaml): `etl:` holds
the run settings and the post-stages, every other top-level key except `_defaults` and `sources` is a
raw table whose rows become MEDS events, and `sources:` says where the raw data lives. The
`MEDS_extract.pipelines` entry point in `pyproject.toml` registers it as `{{ dataset_name }}`, and
MEDS-Extract's `meds-extract-run` does the rest. After the canonical stages, the spec runs, in this
order:

1. `sumeds_link_unit_siblings`: codes that differ only in their unit get the code without it as a parent;
2. `sumeds_normalize_units`: every `unit` in UCUM;
3. this ETL's own post-stages, if any;
4. `sumeds_enrich`: vocabulary tags, titles and parents (see below).

