## Testing

The release cannot be used in CI, so
[`tests/conftest.py`](tests/conftest.py) writes a tiny, fully synthetic input in its layout:

- [`tests/e2e_synthetic_test.py`](tests/e2e_synthetic_test.py) runs `meds-extract-run spec={{ dataset_name }}` over it without the enrichment stage and checks the output;
- [`tests/test_config.py`](tests/test_config.py) checks that the spec loads and that every event describes its codes;
- [`tests/test_sumeds_rules.py`](tests/test_sumeds_rules.py) and
    [`tests/test_run_with_enrichment.py`](tests/test_run_with_enrichment.py) are suMEDS's shared contract
    tests (`sumeds.testing.contracts`, see suMEDS's
    [Testing an ETL](https://github.com/rvandewater/suMEDS/blob/main/docs/testing.md)). They check the
    rules, the post-stage order, and the enrichment against a tiny local ontology source, offline,
    including its soft failure, `suMEDS-enrich` retry and `strict`.
{%- if has_demo %}
- [`tests/e2e_demo_test.py`](tests/e2e_demo_test.py) runs the whole ETL on the open demo release.
{%- endif %}

The [Synthetic ETL workflow](.github/workflows/synthetic-etl.yaml) also generates raw files from the
spec with [MESSY-synth](https://pypi.org/project/MESSY-synth/) and runs the ETL over them. All of these
check the spec's mechanics, not its agreement with the real release; never commit raw data or MEDS output
derived from it.

```bash
uv sync --group dev
uv run pre-commit install
uv run pytest tests/ -v
uv run pre-commit run --all-files
```

## Summary statistics

`tests/regression/full_summary_stats.json` is a
[meds_summary_stats](https://github.com/Medical-Event-Data-Standard/meds_summary_stats) fingerprint of
the MEDS dataset this ETL builds from the full release: counts, code-vocabulary digests, value and time
distributions, code-metadata coverage, splits and schema. It names no code and reports no cell of fewer
than 20 subjects and no minimum or maximum, which is what makes it safe to commit. Its diff shows what
an ETL change did to the output. Regenerate it in every pull request that changes the output, from a
build of that branch:

```bash
# Build the release with this checkout, then summarize it
scripts/summary_stats.sh full --input-dir $RAW_INPUT_DIR
# Or summarize a build of this checkout you already have
scripts/summary_stats.sh full --meds-dir $MEDS_OUTPUT_DIR
```

Both print how the statistics changed against the committed baseline before overwriting it, and refuse
a build whose enrichment failed. Add `--compare` to check a build without writing anything; it exits 1
when [`tests/regression/policy.yaml`](tests/regression/policy.yaml) flags an error. The
[Summary stats workflow](.github/workflows/summary-stats.yaml) warns when a pull request changes `src/`
without regenerating the baseline.

## Releasing

Set up a [PyPI trusted publisher](https://docs.pypi.org/trusted-publishers/) for this repository (see
[the template's instructions](https://github.com/Medical-Event-Data-Standard/ETL_MEDS_Template#pypi)),
then push a version tag (e.g. `git tag 0.1.0 && git push origin 0.1.0`): the release workflow builds,
publishes to PyPI and creates a signed GitHub release. Optionally archive releases on
[Zenodo](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/archiving-a-github-repository/referencing-and-citing-content)
for a citable DOI, and update `CITATION.cff`.

This repository was generated from the
[ETL_MEDS_Template](https://github.com/Medical-Event-Data-Standard/ETL_MEDS_Template) copier template;
run `uvx copier update` (with a clean git tree) to pull in template improvements.

